{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":27,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.rademacher_cannot_distinguish","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.rademacher_cannot_distinguish","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L218-L227","doc":"**Both anchors are forced to the same value, so `Φ` cannot separate them.**\nThe probe reported that `Φ` vanishes for both and \"cannot even distinguish\nthem\"; this is why.  Any two powers of `RL` — the muon and proton closures\nincluded — carry equal `Φ`, whatever `Φ` is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.rademacher_eq_zero_of_homogeneous","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.rademacher_eq_zero_of_homogeneous","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L210-L216","doc":"The same conclusion for a caller who has homogeneity rather than\nantisymmetry — the usual packaging of the Rademacher function. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.rademacher_eq_zero_of_reversible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.rademacher_eq_zero_of_reversible","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L195-L208","doc":"**`Φ((RL)ⁿ) = 0`, for every `n`, from structure rather than computation.**\n\n`Φ` is any conjugation-invariant, inversion-antisymmetric real function on\n`SL(2,ℤ)` — Rademacher's function is one, as is every homogeneous\nquasimorphism.  No Dedekind sum, no word count, no reciprocity law.\n\nThis is the Lean form of the scope-(a) refutation in\n`qbeta_rdmr_rademacher_winding_probe.py`: the Rademacher route cannot supply\n`ϑ_K`, because it is pinned to `0` on the canonical closures. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.reversible_RL_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.reversible_RL_pow","kind":"theorem","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L190-L191","doc":"**Every power of `RL` is reversible**, by the same conjugator `S`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.reversible_RL","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.reversible_RL","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L187-L188","doc":"`RL` is reversible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm_not_conj_Rm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm_not_conj_Rm","kind":"theorem","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L168-L185","doc":"**Control: the same conjugation FAILS for the parabolic `R`.**\n`S · R · S⁻¹ ≠ R⁻¹`, decided.\n\nThis is what makes `Sm_conj_RL` a fact about `RL` rather than a formality that\nany matrix would satisfy — the identity is special to the hyperbolic `RL`, and\nthe vanishing it forces is correspondingly special.\n\nNote the honest limit of this statement: it says THIS conjugator fails for `R`,\nnot that `R` is non-reversible. The latter is true in `SL(2, ℤ)` (the parabolic\nclasses `!![1,n;0,1]` for `n > 0` and `n < 0` are distinct, though they fuse in\n`GL(2, ℤ)`), but proving it needs an argument over ALL conjugators and is not\nattempted here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm_conj_RL","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm_conj_RL","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L165-L166","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm_conj_RL_inv_free","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm_conj_RL_inv_free","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L157-L163","doc":"**The reversing conjugation, explicitly.**  `S · RL · S⁻¹ = (RL)⁻¹`.\n\nProved without inverting anything: `S · RL · S' = RL'` for the explicit\n`S' = !![0,-1;1,0]` and `RL' = !![1,-1;-1,2]`, together with `S · S' = 1` and\n`RL · RL' = 1`, which identify `S'` and `RL'` as the inverses. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RL_eq_Rm_mul_Lm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RL_eq_Rm_mul_Lm","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L151-L155","doc":"`RL` really is `R * L` — so the object below is the braid image, not an\nunrelated matrix that happens to have trace 3. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RmInv_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RmInv_eq","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L149-L149","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RLInv_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RLInv_eq","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L147-L147","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.SmInv_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.SmInv_eq","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L145-L145","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Rm_mul_RmInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Rm_mul_RmInv","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L142-L143","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RL_mul_RLInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RL_mul_RLInv","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L139-L140","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm_mul_SmInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm_mul_SmInv","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L136-L137","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RmInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RmInv","kind":"def","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L133-L134","doc":"`R⁻¹ = !![1,-1;0,1]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RLInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RLInv","kind":"def","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L130-L131","doc":"`(RL)⁻¹ = !![1,-1;-1,2]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.SmInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.SmInv","kind":"def","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L127-L128","doc":"`S⁻¹ = !![0,-1;1,0]`, named so the identities below stay inverse-free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Sm","kind":"def","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L124-L125","doc":"`S = !![0,1;-1,0]`, the order-4 elliptic generator. This is the conjugator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RL","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.RL","kind":"def","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L120-L122","doc":"`RL = !![2,1;1,1]` — the `SL(2,ℤ)` image of the canonical 3-braid\n`σ₁σ₂⁻¹`, and the element whose powers are the two anchors. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Lm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Lm","kind":"def","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L117-L118","doc":"`L = !![1,0;1,1]`, the other parabolic generator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Rm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Rm","kind":"def","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L114-L115","doc":"`R = !![1,1;0,1]`, the parabolic generator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.homogeneous_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.homogeneous_inv","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L102-L110","doc":"**Antisymmetry comes free from homogeneity.**  A homogeneous quasimorphism\nsatisfies `Φ(gⁿ) = n·Φ(g)` for all integers `n`; at `n = -1` that is exactly\n`Φ(g⁻¹) = -Φ(g)`.  So a caller who has homogeneity need not assume `hinv`\nseparately. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.eq_zero_of_reversible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.eq_zero_of_reversible","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L89-L100","doc":"**The vanishing lemma.**  Any conjugation-invariant, inversion-antisymmetric\nreal-valued function vanishes on a reversible element.  Two lines: invariance\nmoves `Φ g` to `Φ g⁻¹`, antisymmetry turns that into `-Φ g`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Reversible.pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Reversible.pow","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L73-L87","doc":"**Reversibility passes to powers.**  Conjugation by a fixed `x` is a group\nhomomorphism, so a single witness `x` reverses every power of `g` at once.\nThis is what makes the result uniform in `n`, where the compute could only\ncheck finitely many. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Reversible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.Reversible","kind":"def","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L70-L71","doc":"An element is **reversible** when it is conjugate to its own inverse. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.SL2Z","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.RademacherReversible.SL2Z","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RademacherReversible.lean#L62-L64","doc":"The modular group `SL(2, ℤ)`. Named explicitly rather than via the scoped\n`SL(2, ℤ)` notation, which this import set does not bring into scope. ","signatureChunk":0}]}